  [1m3. Data flow[0m

  On submit: the handler calls [38;5;153mSession.getUserId()[39m (null on a first-ever visit), passes it to [38;5;153mlogin[39m as [38;5;153mpreviousUserId[39m,
  and on success calls [38;5;153mSession.setUserId(result.userId)[39m. From then on, any form can call [38;5;153mSession.getUserId()[39m and get a
  value without knowing anything about login.

  [38;5;153mSession.clear()[39m is the logout path — but this app has no logout, so nothing will call it yet. I'm including it because
  "cleared on explicit logout" was part of the persistence decision and leaving the function out would mean whoever
  adds logout has to reopen this file. I am [3mnot[0m adding a logout button; that's outside what you asked for.

  [1m4. Error handling and testing[0m

  [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m throws rather than returning null when it's disabled — Safari private mode, some enterprise policies. All
  three functions wrap access in [38;5;153mtry[39m/[38;5;153mcatch[39m and fall back to an in-memory value, so the app degrades to "works for this
  page load" instead of breaking the login form outright. [38;5;153mgetUserId()[39m returns [38;5;153mnull[39m for both "never set" and "storage
  unavailable"; callers treat them identically.

  Testing is the open question: [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m has no dependencies, no [38;5;153mtest[39m script, and there are no tests anywhere. Since
  this repo has no tooling configured at all, it's worth deciding now — it's cheapest before the code exists, and
  [38;5;153msession.js[39m is genuinely unit-testable pure logic.
